Z||000006||West Coast Fiero||12-04-2001||09:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B> <BR>I bet he really appreciates the report.<P>BTW - Cliff provides us with the e-mail icon at the top of each page.  Instead of a long repeat of the original post, try it, you'll like it! <P>In the meantime, I'm betting on a vacuum leak.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>same here, it does sond like it could possibly be a vacum leak at the egr soleniod - allthough I hate long distance prognosis'<P>forgot about the whole email icon on the posts, I get confused with all the different forums that I am on<P>I am researching ALLDATA right now and will have to you everything you should need to repair your current problem<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000007||Archie||12-04-2001||09:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jasons85GT:<BR><B><BR>Things I've replaced: <BR>1) IAC<BR>2) TPS<BR>3) Controle module (under distributor)<BR>4) Distributor and rotor<BR>5) EGR valve<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I alway like to look at the simple things first.<P>In one of your posts you state that you are not mechanically inclined. So it occurs to me to ask..... Did you adjust the TPS, IAC etc. when you put them in?<P>Archie<P>------------------<BR>Just how fast do you want to go?||||reg||1
Z||000008||California Kid||12-04-2001||10:00 PM||||Here's one more thing to check, make sure the port inside your throttle body isn't restricted by carbon build up. There are some ports on the back side of the throttle body also, but you have to remove in from the manifold (if you do, make sure the rubber "O" ring is in good condition (not gouged or torn in the port at the 6 o'clock position.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/californiakidsig_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||cadaver||12-05-2001||01:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B> [QUOTE]Originally posted by Jasons85GT:<BR>[b]Someone told me that my timing could be off....this true??....'85 GT 2.8 Automatic</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>It <B>is</B> possible the ignition timing is off... maybe because the timing chain slipped a notch as you approached the stop light.  Find someone with a timing light that knows what they're doing, follow the procedure on the underhood emissions sticker.  That would be my first step.<P>[/B][/QUOTE]<BR>NO the timing didnt slip a notch when you stopped. That cant happen. If it is worn enough to slip then it stripps or breaks. And that you would know. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||12-05-2001||02:47 PM||||cant say about Fieros for sure. But I HAVE had cars jump one tooth on the timing chain before. Id be looking for a vacuum leak too, anything, maybe even bumped the cruise control vacuum line off. Did you examine the egr tube when you replaced the egr?||||reg||1
